      "problem": "Factor the expression ${y}^{5}-14{y}^{4}+49{y}^{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${y}^{3}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${y}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5}-14{y}^{4}+49{y}^{3} & ={y}^{3}({y}^{2}-14y+49)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression ${y}^{2}-14y+49$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}({y}^{2}-14y+49) & = \\\\ {y}^{3}({y}^{2}-14y+{7}^{2}) & = \\\\ {y}^{3}({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 7\\cdot y+{7}^{2}) & = \\\\ {y}^{3}(y-7)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
